.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_BEGIN 3 2026-09-25 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_begin \- begin a filesystem MPQ update transaction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <libmpq/mpq.h>
.sp
.BI "int32_t libmpq__update_begin("
.BI "        mpq_update_s **" "update,"
.BI "        const char *" "path"
.BI ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a same-directory private working copy of an existing regular MPQ or
container with an embedded MPQ at \fIpath\fP. The original file is unchanged
until commit. Discoverable embedded prefixes and original MPQ-header positions
are preserved. Ordinary legacy v1 and v2 updates also retain supported unrelated
trailing bytes; named reconstruction rejects unrelated trailers. Stale weak
and strong signatures are removed on modification. On
failure, \fI*update\fP is NULL. MPQE archives require
\fBlibmpq__update_begin_mpqe\fP(3). The handle is consumed by commit or abort.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
MPQ v2 can store member positions at or beyond 4 GiB relative to the MPQ
header. The hi-block table supplies 16 high bits in addition to the low
32 bits in the block table. Reading uses the actual source bounds. Creation
can produce sparse output where the backing storage supports it, and stores
the low 32 bits of the archive extent in the legacy size field.
.PP
Begin copies the source into a private stage and can expand sparse regions
on disk. A successful begin leaves mutation and compaction eligibility to
the individual operation. Ordinary legacy table rewrites
require the rebuilt MPQ extent, excluding the prefix and suffix, to fit in
UINT32_MAX bytes and return LIBMPQ_ERROR_SIZE if it does not.
Named reconstruction, including exact data and path replacement, checks the extent
declared by the header against the stage size and permits only a recognized
strong-signature trailer after that extent. An inconsistent extent or
unsupported trailer can return LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT, including when the v2
size field wraps. See
.BR libmpq__update_replace_ranges (3)
and
.BR libmpq__update_compact (3)
for their specific checks.
.PP
MPQ v3 and v4 mutations use available filenames and stored lookup identities.
Unrelated unnamed entries can be retained with fixed classic probe slots or
complete normalized 64-bit HET/BET hashes and consistent row provenance.
Classic hash capacity cannot grow without the missing probe-start hashes.
Unknown encrypted members retain their original offsets and packed bytes;
no filename key is guessed. Partial listfiles remain partial.
.PP
Reduced or ambiguous hashes, incompatible aliases, BET-only classic attributes,
and patch members remain rejected without modifying the staged archive. Rebuilds retain their version and regenerate metadata.
.PP
Embedded support follows normal discovery: aligned MPQ origins or a validated
MPQ user-data pointer, including unaligned pointer targets. Every prefix byte,
user-data field and the original absolute MPQ-header position are retained.
KEY uses MPQ-relative offsets, excluding the prefix. No arbitrary container
repair is performed. Recognized strong trailers follow the existing signature
policy. Validated no-ops preserve complete stage bytes and signatures.
Embedded reconstruction needs both a completed zero-origin MPQ temporary and
a full prefixed candidate before adoption. MPQE uses decoded prefix bytes and
the existing MPQE re-encryption and publication path.
Invalidated signatures are removed. Unchanged commits and aborts require no
reconstruction. V4 preserves the source raw-chunk size, including zero, and
regenerates structural and raw MD5 digests. Retained packed representations
are preserved without recompression; known encrypted relocation adjusts keys.
.PP
Repeated names differing only by ASCII case or slash direction are accepted.
Ambiguous names, embedded null bytes and unresolvable listfile entries reject
reconstruction. A staged close failure prevents further mutation or publication;
abort remains safe and attempts cleanup.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
/* In sample.mpq, docs/old.txt exists and docs/new.txt does not. */
int32_t
rename_in_sample_archive(void)
{
    mpq_update_s *update = NULL;
    int32_t status = libmpq__update_begin(
        &update, "sample.mpq"
    );
    if (status != 0)
        return status;

    status = libmpq__update_rename(update, "docs/old.txt", "docs/new.txt");
    if (status != 0) {
        (void)libmpq__update_abort(update); /* Preserve the rename error. */
        return status;
    }
    return libmpq__update_commit(update); /* Consumes the handle. */
}
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns \fB0\fP on success, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
Invalid paths, unsupported formats, allocation, and backing I/O errors are
returned as negative LIBMPQ_ERROR_* values.
